### What is ReadWriteLock?

ReentrantReadWriteLock separates read operations from write operations to allow higher concurrency for read-heavy workloads. Multiple threads can hold the read lock simultaneously, but the write lock is exclusive: it blocks all readers and other writers.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class BalanceCacheRWL {
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock rwl = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public BigDecimal getBalance() {
        rwl.readLock().lock();
        try {
            return balance;  // 100 threads can be here simultaneously
        } finally {
            rwl.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void updateBalance(BigDecimal newBalance) {
        rwl.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            balance = newBalance;  // only ONE thread, all readers blocked
        } finally {
            rwl.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

### When does ReadWriteLock help?

It helps when reads vastly outnumber writes. With a simple ReentrantLock, 100 read operations serialize: one at a time. With ReadWriteLock, all 100 run in parallel because the read lock is shared.

### When does ReadWriteLock hurt?

When writes are frequent (50:50), the write lock constantly blocks all readers, and the CAS overhead on the shared reader count adds contention even between readers. A simple ReentrantLock has lower per-operation overhead and performs better.

### How does write-to-read downgrade work?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class DowngradeDemo {
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock rwl = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    private BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;

    public BigDecimal updateAndRead(BigDecimal amount) {
        rwl.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            balance = balance.add(amount);
            rwl.readLock().lock();   // acquire read WHILE holding write
        } finally {
            rwl.writeLock().unlock();  // release write, now hold only read
        }
        try {
            return balance;  // still holding read lock, other readers can join
        } finally {
            rwl.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

### Why is read-to-write upgrade not allowed?

```java
// WRONG: read-to-write upgrade causes DEADLOCK
rwl.readLock().lock();
rwl.writeLock().lock();  // DEADLOCK!
// Write lock waits for ALL readers to release.
// But THIS thread is a reader and will not release until it gets the write lock.
// Circular dependency: deadlock.
```

The correct approach is to release the read lock first, acquire the write lock, then re-check the condition.

### How do you choose between lock types?

| Workload | Recommended lock | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| 99:1 read:write | StampedLock optimistic | Zero CAS, zero contention |
| 95:5 read:write | ReentrantReadWriteLock | Parallel reads, occasional write blocking |
| 50:50 | ReentrantLock | Simplest, lowest overhead |
| Low contention | synchronized | JVM optimizes heavily |

### What about fairness?

Fair mode (`new ReentrantReadWriteLock(true)`) uses FIFO ordering: the longest-waiting thread gets the lock next. This prevents writer starvation (where a continuous stream of readers prevents writers from ever acquiring the lock). Non-fair mode (default) allows barging, where a thread may acquire the lock ahead of waiting threads. Barging improves throughput but can starve individual threads.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you hold both read and write locks at the same time? Yes, during downgrade: hold write lock, acquire read lock, release write lock. But NOT during upgrade.
- Is ReadWriteLock better than ConcurrentHashMap? ConcurrentHashMap uses per-bucket locking which is usually sufficient. ReadWriteLock is for custom data structures or cross-structure operations.
